From 7bad1c942c0fc697450930c06e81ec446b47231a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 1 Aug 2026 19:46:55 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Give compilation a stack the engine sizes

The scanner, the parser, the syntax validator and the IL emitter each
recurse once per level of source nesting, so how deeply nested a script the
front end accepts was a function of whatever stack the host thread happened
to be created with - 1 MiB on a Windows main thread, 8 MiB on a typical
Linux one, and not knowable from managed code. Past that depth the process
does not fail, it aborts: a stack overflow is not a catchable exception on
.NET, so a syntactically valid script takes the host down with it and no try
around the compile call can help.

Source *size* is not what does it, which is what the roadmap item had
recorded. A flat 200 000-statement function compiles and runs. Nesting is,
and on the script host's 16 MiB thread three passes overflow in turn, each
reached once the one before it is given more stack: FastParser's recursive
descent on a right-nested conditional, AstReduce under
SyntaxValidation.StrictModeValidator on a left-nested + chain - the abort
trace shows ~19 400 levels of its three-frame cycle at ~845 B each - and
ILCodeGenerator.VisitBinary once the front end survives. Because the cost
per level is constant, the same shapes abort at ~1 200 levels on a 1 MiB
stack and ~9 700 on an 8 MiB one.

CompilationStack runs one compilation on a thread it sizes itself, the
compile-time counterpart of the script host sizing its execution thread. It
is deliberately not the same budget as MaxStackUsageBytes: JavaScript
recursion depth is observable to the program, compiler recursion depth is
not, so raising the second changes no program's meaning - and until now a
script whose source merely nested deeply was charged against the budget
meant for a script that recurses. The boundary sits where each ICodeCache
starts a compilation, so parse, validation, tree construction and emission
share one crossing, with catch-alls in CoreScript and RuntimeAssembly for a
host that brings its own cache.

Two things keep it affordable, and both were measured rather than assumed.
Workers are parked and reused: a thread per compilation costs ~300 us,
because reserving a large stack is a kernel mapping rather than a
bookkeeping entry, and that measured +27% on a compile-only loop. And a
source under 512 characters compiles where it stands - nesting depth cannot
exceed source length, so that much source cannot exhaust even a 1 MiB stack
- which keeps the fixed ~180 us handoff off the smallest and most frequent
compilations there are, where it would be a larger cost than the compile.
After both, measured ABBA-interleaved at process granularity over five pairs
of a 5 000-compile loop with BROILER_JS_COMPILE_STACK_BYTES as the only
difference between the two sides: +1.5% by median of paired ratios, +3.2%
comparing medians, one pair of five negative.

StackGuard is the mechanism that should already have done this and cannot
fire. It tests address - start > MaxStackSize on a stack that grows
downwards, so the difference is negative and the segmenting branch is
unreachable; it also truncates stack addresses to int, and would hop every
1 024 bytes if it did fire. CallFrames.EnsureWithinStackBudget gets the
direction right and says so in a comment. Repairing StackGuard, giving it a
threshold in megabytes and handing its segments a sized thread rather than a
thread-pool thread is the real fix; this buys time for it rather than
replacing it, and says so where it is defined.

DeeplyNestedSourceTests pins the four shapes, including the long flat one so
that the cause cannot be re-diagnosed from source length again. They assert
values rather than exceptions because the failure being pinned is not a
thrown error but the test host aborting: with
BROILER_JS_COMPILE_STACK_BYTES=0 the run does not fail, it dies in
FastParser.
